INT. FIELD HOSPITAL TENT - NIGHT

Canvas walls breathing in the wind. A generator rattles outside. Kerosene lamps cast long shadows over a row of cots, most empty.

DR. SOLEDAD ARRIAGA (40s, sleeves rolled to the elbow, hair clipped under a cap) leans over a cot where a young soldier lies unconscious, his chest rising in shallow, uneven breaths. Her hands move with the economy of long practice: a pulse at the wrist, a lifted eyelid, a pressed palm on the sternum.

Opposite her, a nurse, SISTER AGNES KOWALSKI (50s, wimple, a rosary peeking from her apron pocket), wrings out a cloth in a basin and lays it on the boy's forehead.

SOLEDAD
How long since the last dose?

AGNES
Forty minutes.

SOLEDAD
And his pressure?

AGNES
Falling.

Soledad takes a breath. Straightens. Her gaze drifts to the tent flap, where the wind has begun to tug at the canvas ties.

SOLEDAD
Prepare the table. We're opening him.

AGNES
Doctor, without plasma...

SOLEDAD
Then without plasma.

Agnes hesitates, her fingers at the rosary. Then she moves, quick and quiet, to the instrument tray.

The tent flap tears open. A gust of cold air swirls through the tent, snuffing two of the lamps. In the doorway stands a tall, soaked figure, rain streaming from the brim of his helmet.

COLONEL AUGUSTIN RENARD (50s, medals dulled by mud, one arm in a sling) takes a step inside. Behind him, nothing but darkness and the sound of far-off guns.

RENARD
Doctor Arriaga.

SOLEDAD
Colonel. This is not a good moment.

RENARD
It is the only moment I have.

He glances at the boy on the cot. His jaw tightens.

RENARD (CONT'D)
That one is mine.

SOLEDAD
Then you may watch.

Agnes holds out the scalpel. Soledad takes it. Renard removes his helmet and stands very still while the generator coughs and the lamps flicker, and the first incision is made.
